<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khmelnytsky Uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1648">1648</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khmelnytsky Uprising was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> rebellion that began in 1648 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> in the Ukrainian lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Drawing on Cossack, peasant and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> grievances against Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, it inflicted heavy defeats on Commonwealth forces. The revolt led eventually to Russian involvement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and opened a period of wars that severely weakened the Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising.jpg" alt="Khmelnytsky Uprising" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mykola Ivasiuk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pic_I_V_Ivasiuk_Mykola_Bohdan_Khmelnytskys_Entry_to_Kyiv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1569 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> gave the Ruthenian voivodeships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>, Bracław and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-the-kingdom-of-poland">Crown of Poland</a> within the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Bohdan Khmelnytsky was born to a noble family and attended a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> school, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>. After service against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and a period of captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he returned as a Registered Cossack and settled at his estate of Subotiv. His life changed when Aleksander Koniecpolski, heir of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> estate, tried to seize his land. In 1647 Daniel Czapliński harassed Khmelnytsky on Koniecpolski's behalf and raided Subotiv twice, and his son Yurii was badly beaten. Appeals to the king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 25 January 1648 Khmelnytsky and 400 to 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> attacked the Sich on Mykytyn Rih. Registered Cossacks joined him, and the Cossack Rada elected him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> by the end of the month. He allied with the Crimean Khanate. In April and May Cossack and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> forces won the first victories. Talks in June under Adam Kysil led to Khmelnytsky's demands for more Registered Cossacks and the return of churches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>. The Commonwealth army of 35,000 to 40,000, encumbered by a baggage train, was routed at Pyliavtsi on 23 September 1648. In January 1649 Khmelnytsky entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in triumph.</p><p>Fighting resumed in 1649 and 1651, and in June 1652 at Batih a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> force killed the field hetman Marcin Kalinowski and half of his hussars. On 11 October 1653 the Zemsky Sobor of Muscovy decided to accept the Zaporozhian Host under the tsar, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslav</a> followed in early 1654. The Swedish invasion of the Commonwealth in 1655, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>, further changed the war. Sources disagree on when the uprising ended: 1654, 1655 or Khmelnytsky's death in 1657.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The uprising is generally considered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> victory, since it ended Polish-Lithuanian rule over much of the Ukrainian lands and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a>. The struggle to consolidate the victory, outside pressure and divisions after Khmelnytsky's death led to a period known as the Ruin. It eventually led to the incorporation of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> into the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and with the Swedish and Russian invasions it ended the Polish Golden Age.</p><p>The war brought mass atrocities against civilians, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Catholic and Uniate clergy, by rebels, and reprisals by the Polish army and nobility. Jews, who often leased estates, were seen by peasants as immediate oppressors, and many Jews regard these events as the biggest national catastrophe since the destruction of the Temple. Population loss in the whole Commonwealth from 1648 to 1667, which includes later wars, is estimated at about 4 million.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khmelnytsky Uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cossack Hetmanate, officially the Zaporozhian Host, was a stratocratic state built by Registered Cossacks in Dnieper Ukraine out of the <b>Khmelnytsky Uprising</b> of 1648 against the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Khmelnytsky Uprising</b>, the Cossack revolt led by Bohdan Khmelnytsky from 1648, inflicted catastrophic losses on Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Khmelnytsky Uprising</b> against the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, after his victories over the Poles, Bohdan Khmelnytsky entered Kyiv at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
